{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["Kaftorim — this is the passage of those terefot in the Gemara: the Avvim came from Aḥuzat ha-Dam, from Teiman. And why were they called Avvim? Because they bent (עיוו) their place. Another matter: because they desired (איוו) many divinities, and alef interchanges with ayin. Another matter: because whoever saw them was seized by an עוית — that is, an illness, and they were given that name. And in Bereshit Rabbah: Avvim — because they ensnared the world, and because they were ensnared from the world, and caused the world to be ensnared; as it is written: עוה עוה עוה אשימנה. R. Elazar bar R. Shimon says: because they were expert in dusts like a snake — in the Galilee they cry out to a snake: עיויא." "Kaftorim, who came forth from Kaftor, destroyed them and dwelt in their stead — so you too shall know that the Holy One, blessed be He, will give you as inheritance that which He swore to your fathers."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The opening words פרק אלו טרפות בגמ׳ likely refer to a specific sugya; I render the phrase literally. עפרות כנחש is read as 'dusts like a snake' following the comparison; the Galilean dialect cry עיויא is left transliterated."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:38:16.989107819Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}